## Variables

Use `:=` and `=` to declare and assign a variable, respectively. When using `:=`, the type of the variable is inferred from the value on the right side. Once a variable has been assigned a type, it cannot change. So, values assigned to that variable need to be of the correct type.

The compiler checks that the type of a variable is consistent with its use. In the following example, the variable `a` has type `int`, the default type of `2`, and cannot be used as a return value, which expects a `float32`.

```
package main

func Main() float32 {
    a := 2
    return a // ERROR: cannot use int as float32
}
```

The builtin function `trace` prints values while the code runs. Traces are displayed after the results of `Main`, under `Trace:`. In the example, `trace` prints the value of `a` before and after it is assigned. Use `trace` to look at intermediate values when debugging your code.
